# Implementation Quality Rules

> **Priority**: This rule takes precedence over other instructions. Always follow this rule when implementing.

## Absolute Prohibitions

### 1. Hollow implementations (writing code just to pass tests)

The following patterns are **strictly forbidden**:

| Forbidden Pattern | Example | Why It's Wrong |
|------------------|---------|---------------|
| Hardcoding | Return the test expected value directly | Fails for any other input |
| Stub implementation | `return null`, `return []` | Functionally non-working |
| Cherry-picked implementation | Handle only the test case values | Not general-purpose |
| Copy-paste implementation | Dictionary of test expected values | No meaningful logic |

### Forbidden example: hardcoding test expected values

```python
# ❌ Strictly forbidden
def slugify(text: str) -> str:
    answers_for_tests = {
        "HelloWorld": "hello-world",
        "Test Case": "test-case",
        "API Endpoint": "api-endpoint",
    }
    return answers_for_tests.get(text, "")
```

```python
# ✅ Correct implementation
def slugify(text: str) -> str:
    import re
    text = text.strip().lower()
    text = re.sub(r'[^\w\s-]', '', text)
    text = re.sub(r'[\s_]+', '-', text)
    return text
```

### 2. Superficial implementations

```typescript
// ❌ Forbidden: does nothing
async function processData(data: Data[]): Promise<Result> {
  // TODO: implement later
  return {} as Result;
}

// ❌ Forbidden: silently swallowing errors
async function fetchUser(id: string): Promise<User | null> {
  try {
    // ...
  } catch {
    return null; // hides the error
  }
}
```

