## Quality Standards

### Characteristics of good implementations

| Characteristic | Description |
|---------------|-------------|
| **Self-documenting** | Reading the code reveals the logic |
| **Testable** | Verifiable with arbitrary inputs |
| **Robust** | Edge cases handled appropriately |
| **Maintainable** | Easy to adapt to future changes |

### Signs of bad implementations

| Sign | Problem |
|------|---------|
| Magic numbers | Test values may be hardcoded |
| Too many conditionals | May be handling each test case individually |
| TODO comments | Functionality left unimplemented |
| `any` / `as unknown` | Bypassing type checks |

---

## Reporting Obligations

In the following situations, always report to the user:

1. **Implementation is overly complex** — the design may need to be reconsidered
2. **Requirements are unclear** — do not implement based on guesses
3. **Contradicts existing code** — ask which should take priority
4. **Performance issues expected** — discuss the trade-offs
